# The whole set, resolved into one schema.
#
# This is not a layer — it declares nothing of its own. It is what you pass to `fjord create`
# for a single database holding every layer:
#
#     fjord --schema-path ./schemas create index --schema ./schemas/index.sigla
#
# Composing lets a query join across layers, which separate databases cannot do: a `FactId`
# does not leave the database that issued it ([I11](invariants.html#i11)). Both of these run
# against a database created from this file —
#
#     # Every reference in one file, resolved to its definition, for any language present.
#     {use = SP, target = S, defFile = DF, def = D.value} where
#       F = src.File "packages/app/src/App.tsx";
#       codemarkup.FileXRef {file = F, span = SP, target = S, role = R};
#       D = codemarkup.Definition {symbol = S, file = DF}
#
#     # A symbol, its declaration site, and the project that compiled the file it sits in.
#     {def = D.value, proj = P.value} where
#       S = src.Symbol "scip-csharp nuget Company.Domain 1.4.0 Company/Domain/Order#Total().";
#       D = codemarkup.Definition {symbol = S, file = F};
#       msbuild.SourceFileToProject {src = F, project = P}
#
# The trade is that one database is one write window and one failure domain: a producer that
# dies half-way leaves a half-filled composite, and a completed database is immutable, so
# every producer must finish before any of them is served. The usual shape is one composed
# database per repository, with a fan-out across repositories joined on `src.Symbol`.

schema index {

  import src
  import config
  import codemarkup
  import msbuild
  import csharp
  import npm
  import bundle
  import typescript

}
